[Analytics](/systems/analytics) is a second running example: `packages/events` owns capture and its Basin resources. Its removal checklist is explicit in [README](/README.md), rather than a claim that deleting one import removes everything. `packages/subscriber-delivery` is a provider adapter behind core's ports, not a proven one-line infrastructure cartridge.

## Where the pattern comes from

"Cartridge" is this project's metaphor, not Sam Goodwin's wording. Sam describes Layers as a way to carry infrastructure and permissions with a service, and calls the idea "SaaS as NPM." [devtools.fm, 21:15](https://youtu.be/MtKHzAH6uXM?t=1275). He also describes swapping implementations by providing different Layers. [Effect Office Hours 27, 34:35](https://youtu.be/fPSxB7ZgSJw?t=2075).

The recordings support the mechanism, not every vendor being interchangeable. Sam describes test or development Layers for infrastructure and a type error when code still requires a removed binding. [Happy Path 116, 15:25](https://youtu.be/YEn4A7XxgF4?t=925) [Effect Office Hours 27, 07:05](https://youtu.be/fPSxB7ZgSJw?t=425).

See [one capability, every surface](/lore/one-capability-every-surface) for the shared contract and [a clean playground](/lore/a-clean-playground) for the house rule behind these boundaries.
